WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.470 --> 00:00:07.270
So, the mainframe handles a whole lot of transactions.

2
00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:09.950
And it's important that they be fast and accurate.

3
00:00:09.950 --> 00:00:15.390
But it's also extremely important that the data for these transactions be safe and secure.

4
00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:20.070
Safe to say, in today's information economy, a large-scale data breach is one of the

5
00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:22.630
worst things that can happen.

6
00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:28.150
So how do we make sure that the data in these transactions is kept safe from prying eyes?

7
00:00:28.150 --> 00:00:31.390
One of the most commonly used practices is encryption.

8
00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:37.310
Basically scrambling data in such a way we can control who is able to de-scramble it.

9
00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:41.430
There are a number of encryption algorithms and practices we can use to handle the encryption

10
00:00:41.430 --> 00:00:46.910
and decryption of data, but it all comes down to controlling who has the keys.

11
00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:50.110
In cryptography, a key is just a series of characters.

12
00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:53.830
How many characters generally depends on the strength of the encryption.

13
00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:58.670
If my key is only three bits with each bit, either being a one or a zero, there's

14
00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:02.190
only eight possible ways we can flip those bits.

15
00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:04.870
So not much of a key at all.

16
00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:08.670
Every time you add a bit, you double the amount of possible combinations someone would

17
00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:09.990
have to guess.

18
00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:17.910
So four bits gets you to 16, five bits gets you to 32, fast-forward to 64 bits, and you've

19
00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:21.070
got billions of possible combinations.

20
00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:25.230
But even that's guessable with enough time and dedicated resources.

21
00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:31.470
So for very important data, you'll often see 256 bit and higher levels of encryption.

22
00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:35.670
And those keys will get refreshed from time to time just to minimize the possibility

23
00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.750
of them getting figured out and exploited.

24
00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:43.870
One of the most basic forms of securing data is what's called symmetric encryption.

25
00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:47.950
In this method, there's a key that both encrypts and decrypts data.

26
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:52.390
And you give a copy of that key to anyone who has a need to access the data.

27
00:01:52.390 --> 00:01:56.590
It works, but it's difficult to scale up while keeping it secure, and you also have to

28
00:01:56.590 --> 00:02:00.030
distribute those keys to all the intended users.

29
00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.110
This is where public key cryptography comes in.

30
00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:08.430
In public key cryptography, there are two keys, a public key and a private key.

31
00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:13.150
And their mathematically linked in such a way that the public key can be used to encrypt

32
00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:18.910
a message, and only the private key that's linked to that public key can decrypt it.

33
00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:22.350
So I can give out my public key to pretty much anyone.

34
00:02:22.350 --> 00:02:27.310
They can't use that to figure out my private key, and all it means is they can encrypt

35
00:02:27.310 --> 00:02:31.310
a message in such a way that only I can decrypt it.

36
00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:35.350
And if I want to send a message to my friend, and I want to make sure no one else can decrypt

37
00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:40.230
it, I can encrypt it with their public key, so they can decrypt it with their private

38
00:02:40.230 --> 00:02:41.830
key.

39
00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:43.870
Pop quiz time.

40
00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:44.870
You got that?

41
00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:45.870
Good.

42
00:02:45.870 --> 00:02:50.310
Now, public and private keys can be used for some other pretty interesting things, like

43
00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:55.510
signing documents and creating hashes for data, which reveal if any of the data has been

44
00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:57.150
tampered with.

45
00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:02.230
Both of these are incredibly important functions of cryptography and used extensively

46
00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:03.830
in business.

47
00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:09.190
This whole idea of public and private key infrastructure only works if private keys are

48
00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:11.110
kept private.

49
00:03:11.110 --> 00:03:14.390
That is key.

50
00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:15.390
Guys?

51
00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:23.950
Key?

52
00:03:23.950 --> 00:03:31.870
The IBMZ has dedicated facilities and hardware to enable, accelerate, and further secure

53
00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:34.230
these cryptographic functions.

54
00:03:34.230 --> 00:03:40.350
For starters, there's CPACF, the CP Assist for cryptographic functions.

55
00:03:40.350 --> 00:03:45.710
This is a functionality provided on the processors specifically built largely around symmetric

56
00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:47.390
key functions.

57
00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:53.150
And can greatly speed up things like VPN, SSL, and data storing applications.

58
00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:56.630
There's also the Crypto Express PCIe card.

59
00:03:56.630 --> 00:04:01.190
This is a dedicated card that handles more advanced cryptographic functions.

60
00:04:01.190 --> 00:04:05.950
By offloading cryptographic work to specialize software and hardware, we were able to keep

61
00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:11.910
our keys safe while still making encryption, decryption, and other cryptographic capabilities

62
00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:16.150
readily available to all aspects of Z data processing.

63
00:04:16.150 --> 00:04:22.350
And I do mean all aspects, because starting with the Z14 version of the IBMZ mainframe,

64
00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:27.510
there's a feature called pervasive encryption, which lets us more easily keep data encrypted

65
00:04:27.510 --> 00:04:31.670
while still allowing us and others to manage that data.

66
00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:35.790
That way, it only needs to be decrypted at the moment it's actually needed, minimizing

67
00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:41.870
the possibility of confidential data being seen by people who shouldn't have access.

68
00:04:41.870 --> 00:04:46.950
This feature gets a lot of its functionality from CPACF and Crypto Express.

69
00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:50.670
There are some other important and interesting considerations when it comes to crypto

70
00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:51.990
and practice.

71
00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:55.630
In the next video, you'll hear a little bit more about that from my buddy, Mark.
